# FocusBook Desktop App — Electron + Always-on-Top Widget

## What This Builds

Converts FocusBook into a native desktop application using **Electron**. The existing web app (index.html / style.css / app.js) becomes the main window. A second, always-on-top **overlay widget** lives in the bottom-right corner of the screen: it appears as a small flame icon and expands into a compact task panel after 2 seconds of hover.

---

## Architecture

```
FocusBookV8/
├── index.html          ← existing (main app window, unchanged)
├── style.css           ← existing (unchanged)
├── app.js              ← existing + tiny IPC bridge added
├── package.json        ← NEW — Electron entry, scripts, deps
├── main.js             ← NEW — Electron main process (2 windows)
├── overlay.html        ← NEW — widget markup
├── overlay.css         ← NEW — widget styles + hover animation
└── overlay.js          ← NEW — widget renderer logic + IPC
```

**Two Electron windows:**
| Window | Size | Frame | Transparent | Always on Top |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Main app | 1100×760 | Yes | No | No |
| Overlay widget | 60×60 → 340×460 | No | Yes | Yes (`screen-saver` level) |

**Data flow (IPC):**
- Main window app.js fires `ipcRenderer.send('tasks-update', tasks)` whenever the tasks array changes.
- `main.js` relays the message to the overlay window.
- Overlay renders a compact today-only task list.

